From 22bf6723609f89eeff33c848a779116bfb4e5f0f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Giuseppe Maggio <jertlok@proton.me>
Date: Wed, 16 Sep 2026 20:09:19 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 235/235] media: qcom: camss: offer unpacked 10-bit on RDI for
 every Bayer order

CSID gen1 can write each sample into its own 16-bit word instead of dumping
MIPI-packed data: csid_configure_stream() programs RDI_MODE_PLAIN_PACKING and
PLAIN_FORMAT_16 when the source pad carries a padded code. Only SBGGR10 and
Y10 could ever reach it, because csid_src_pad_code() offers the padded
alternative for those two sink codes alone.

Nothing about the hardware is BGGR-specific -- plain packing is per sample and
does not care which colour a sample is. Offer the padded source code for all
four 10-bit Bayer orders, accept them through the ISPIF, and map them to the
unpacked V4L2 pixel formats at 16 bits per pixel.

This matters beyond tidiness. Packed RAW10 rows are width * 5 / 4 bytes, which
is almost never 64-byte aligned, and a GPU that needs a 64-aligned pitch to
import a dmabuf cannot take the buffer at all -- so a software ISP ends up
copying the whole raw plane every frame. Unpacked rows are width * 2, which is
64-aligned for every even width, and the copy disappears. On a 4032-wide
sensor that is 5040 bytes a row versus 8064.

Assisted-by: Claude
